A semiconductor's fate is largely settled within the first few picoseconds after a
photon is absorbed: carriers thermalise, localise, couple to lattice vibrations, and either
survive to be extracted or are lost.

We use transient absorption and time-resolved photoluminescence spectroscopy to resolve these
dynamics directly, including hot-carrier cooling in perovskite nanocrystals and the ultrafast
carrier localisation that limits transport in NaBiS2. Understanding the coupling between
carriers and phonons tells us which loss channels are intrinsic and which we can engineer away.
